workedExamples.question_text:
The directed network shows pipe capacities in litres per minute.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\usetikzlibrary{decorations.markings}
\tikzset{flow/.style={thick,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.55 with {\arrow{>}}},postaction={decorate}}}
\coordinate (S) at (0,1.5);
\coordinate (A) at (2,3);
\coordinate (B) at (2,0);
\coordinate (T) at (5,1.5);
\draw[flow] (S)--(A) node[midway,above left] {$9$};
\draw[flow] (S)--(B) node[midway,below left] {$12$};
\draw[flow] (B)--(A) node[pos=0.35,left] {$4$};
\draw[flow] (A)--(T) node[midway,above right] {$7$};
\draw[flow] (B)--(T) node[midway,below right] {$8$};
\foreach \v/\p in {S/left,A/above,B/below,T/right}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);\path (\v) node[\p] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(a) Calculate the capacity of the cut separating $\{S,A\}$ from $\{B,T\}$. Explain how edge $BA$ is treated.

(b) Compare every possible valid cut, identify the minimum cut and determine the maximum flow.

(c) Show a flow attaining this value and state the unused capacities of the forward edges crossing the minimum cut.

workedExamples.solution_text:
(a) The forward edges crossing the cut are $SB$ and $AT$.

$\text{Cut capacity}=12+7=19\text{ L/min}$.

Edge $BA$ points from the sink side to the source side, so its capacity is excluded.

(b) Each of the two intermediate vertices can lie on either side. The four possible source-side sets give:

$\begin{aligned}\{S\}:&\quad 9+12=21\\\{S,A\}:&\quad 12+7=19\\\{S,B\}:&\quad 9+4+8=21\\\{S,A,B\}:&\quad 7+8=15\end{aligned}$

The minimum cut crosses $AT$ and $BT$, as shown in red.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\usetikzlibrary{decorations.markings}
\definecolor{cutred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\tikzset{flow/.style={thick,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.55 with {\arrow{>}}},postaction={decorate}}}
\coordinate (S) at (0,1.5);
\coordinate (A) at (2,3);
\coordinate (B) at (2,0);
\coordinate (T) at (5,1.5);
\draw[flow] (S)--(A) node[midway,above left] {$9$};
\draw[flow] (S)--(B) node[midway,below left] {$12$};
\draw[flow] (B)--(A) node[pos=0.35,left] {$4$};
\draw[flow] (A)--(T) node[midway,above right] {$7$};
\draw[flow] (B)--(T) node[midway,below right] {$8$};
\draw[cutred,thick,dashed] (4,-0.3)--(4,3.3);
\foreach \v/\p in {S/left,A/above,B/below,T/right}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);\path (\v) node[\p] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
By the maximum-flow minimum-cut theorem, the maximum flow is $15\text{ L/min}$.

(c) Send $7\text{ L/min}$ along $SAT$ and $8\text{ L/min}$ along $SBT$. These flows respect every capacity and total $15\text{ L/min}$, matching the minimum cut.

Edges $AT$ and $BT$ are fully saturated, so each has unused capacity $0\text{ L/min}$.

foundation.question_text:
A network with source $S$ and sink $T$ is shown.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{decorations.markings}
\tikzset{midarrow/.style={decoration={markings, mark=at position 0.5 with {\arrow{>}}}, postaction={decorate}}}
\coordinate (S) at (0,1.5);
\coordinate (A) at (2.5,2.7);
\coordinate (B) at (2.5,0.3);
\coordinate (T) at (5.0,1.5);
\draw[midarrow] (S) -- (A) node[midway, above left] {$18$};
\draw[midarrow] (S) -- (B) node[midway, below left] {$22$};
\draw[midarrow] (A) -- (B) node[midway, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$6$};
\draw[midarrow] (A) -- (T) node[midway, above right] {$15$};
\draw[midarrow] (B) -- (T) node[midway, below right] {$17$};
\fill (S) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$S$};
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$B$};
\fill (T) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$T$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find the capacity of the cut separating $\{S, A, B\}$ from $\{T\}$, and determine the maximum flow from $S$ to $T$.

